//@version=6
// ============================================================================
//  SMT Divergence ICT, Smart Money Technique & Correlation [LunqFX]
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Two instruments that normally move together stop agreeing. One makes a
//  higher high, the other fails to. That disagreement is the Smart Money
//  Technique divergence, and it is one of the few reversal reads that comes
//  from outside the chart you are trading rather than from the chart itself.
//
//  Every published SMT tool marks the divergence and stops there. Two things
//  here do not:
//
//    1  YOU SEE IT      the correlated symbol's own path is drawn on your
//                       chart, rescaled into your price range, so the moment
//                       the two structures part company is visible rather
//                       than announced after the fact
//    2  IT IS CHECKED   correlation between the two is measured live. SMT on
//                       two instruments that are not actually correlated is
//                       not a signal, it is a coincidence, and the panel says
//                       which of the two you are looking at
//
//  NON-REPAINTING: divergences are built from CONFIRMED pivots and the second
//  symbol is requested on the chart's own timeframe with lookahead off. A mark
//  that has printed never moves.
// ============================================================================
// max_bars_back is declared because the rescaling window and the correlation
// window both reach 500 bars, and a requested series indexed at the pivot
// offset on top of that can push past the default buffer.
indicator("SMT Divergence ICT, Smart Money Technique & Correlation [LunqFX]",
     "SMT", overlay = true, max_lines_count = 500, max_labels_count = 500,
     max_bars_back = 600)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  PALETTE
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
UP    = #00F5D4
DN    = #FF2E88
GHOST = #7C8AA5
INK   = #0B0E14
WARN  = #FBBF24
CARD  = #131A24
CARD2 = #1B2431
TXT   = #F1F5F9
MUTE  = #94A3B8

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  INPUTS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
gS   = "The pair being compared"
autoPair = input.bool(true, "Pick the correlated symbol automatically", group = gS, tooltip = "Chooses the classic SMT partner for the chart's asset class: EURUSD ↔ GBPUSD, BTC ↔ ETH, ES ↔ NQ, gold ↔ silver, SPX ↔ NDX, a stock against SPY. Switch it off to name the symbol yourself below. The panel shows which symbol is in use either way.")
corrIn   = input.symbol("OANDA:GBPUSD", "Correlated symbol (manual)", group = gS, tooltip = "Used only when automatic selection is off. It has to be an instrument that normally moves WITH yours — or exactly against it, inverse pairs are handled. Pick a symbol that is not correlated and every divergence the script finds is noise, which is why the correlation is measured and shown.")
// Defaults set for a clean chart rather than a busy one. Correlated pairs
// disagree at minor swings constantly: at a swing length of eight and a low
// disagreement floor, EURUSD against GBPUSD produces a divergence at almost
// every pivot, which is technically true and useless to look at.
pivLen = input.int(8, "Swing length", minval = 2, maxval = 50, group = gS, tooltip = "How major a swing has to be before it counts as a pivot. Short lengths mark the divergences you can see forming between the two lines; long ones keep only the swing-level ones. Raise it if the chart gets busy.")
minDiv = input.float(0.75, "Minimum disagreement", minval = 0.0, maxval = 3.0, step = 0.05, group = gS, tooltip = "How far the correlated symbol has to travel the OTHER way, measured against its own average bar range. Below about half a range the disagreement is noise. This is the first setting to change if you want more or fewer marks.")

gC   = "Correlation check"
corLen = input.int(100, "Correlation window", minval = 20, maxval = 500, group = gC)
corMin = input.float(0.5, "Warn below", minval = 0.0, maxval = 0.95, step = 0.05, group = gC, tooltip = "Below this the panel turns amber. SMT between two instruments that are not moving together says nothing about smart money — it just says they are different instruments.")
corGate= input.bool(true, "Only mark while correlated", group = gC, tooltip = "On by default. Two instruments that have stopped moving together disagree constantly, and every one of those disagreements would print as a divergence. Switch it off to see them anyway — the panel will still be telling you the reading is not trustworthy.")

gV   = "Visuals"
// One palette cannot suit every background, which is why the most-used candle
// theming scripts ship a shelf of them rather than a single opinion. These are
// schemes that circulate among traders rather than colours invented here.
candSty = input.string("Neon Bloom", "Candle palette", options = ["Neon Bloom","Midnight Cyan","Vibrant Neon","Classic Soft","Cool Blue","Off"], group = gV, tooltip = "Neon Bloom is turquoise against magenta. Midnight Cyan is the common signature dark scheme. Vibrant Neon is the high-contrast crypto pairing, Classic Soft the muted everyday version. Cool Blue drops red entirely — a number of full-time traders run blue against grey because red carries a stress response that gets in the way of reading a chart for eight hours. Off keeps your own candles.")
ghost   = input.bool(true, "Draw the correlated symbol", group = gV, tooltip = "The second symbol's path, rescaled into your chart's price range so the two structures can be compared by eye. It carries no price of its own — only shape.")
ghostW  = input.int(100, "Rescaling window (bars)", minval = 30, maxval = 500, group = gV, tooltip = "How much history the rescaling is fitted to. Shorter tracks recent structure more closely; longer keeps the shape steadier.")
ghostLw = input.int(2, "Correlated path width", minval = 1, maxval = 4, group = gV)
showLink= input.bool(true, "Join the diverging swings", group = gV)
maxSpan = input.int(120, "Longest connector (bars)", minval = 10, maxval = 1000, group = gV, tooltip = "A divergence is always against the previous pivot, however far back that was. Past this distance the connector is not drawn — a dashed line reaching five months across a daily chart tells you nothing — but the mark itself still prints.")
lblSize = input.string("Large", "Mark size", options = ["Small","Normal","Large","Huge"], group = gV, tooltip = "How loudly the divergences are drawn. On a chart that is mostly context you want them big enough to find without hunting.")
showHUD = input.bool(true, "Dashboard", group = gV)
hudPos  = input.string("Top Right", "Dashboard position", options = ["Top Right","Top Left","Bottom Right","Bottom Left","Middle Right"], group = gV)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  THE SECOND SYMBOL
//  Requested on the chart's own timeframe, so there is no higher-timeframe
//  boundary to look across and nothing to repaint. lookahead is left off
//  regardless, because the default is not something a reader should have to
//  take on trust.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A fixed default suits one asset class and looks broken on every other:
// Bitcoin against GBPUSD reads a correlation of nothing and holds every mark.
// So the partner is chosen from the chart's own type — the pairing each
// market's SMT traders actually use — unless the user names one. Metals are
// checked by ticker first because brokers file them as CFDs, not as a class.
tk  = str.upper(syminfo.ticker)
typ = syminfo.type
autoSym = str.contains(tk, "XAU") or str.contains(tk, "GOLD")   ? "OANDA:XAGUSD" :
          str.contains(tk, "XAG") or str.contains(tk, "SILVER") ? "OANDA:XAUUSD" :
          typ == "crypto"  ? (str.contains(tk, "ETH") ? "BINANCE:BTCUSDT" : "BINANCE:ETHUSDT") :
          typ == "forex"   ? (str.contains(tk, "GBP") ? "OANDA:EURUSD" : "OANDA:GBPUSD") :
          typ == "futures" ? (str.contains(tk, "NQ") ? "CME_MINI:ES1!" : "CME_MINI:NQ1!") :
          typ == "index"   ? (str.contains(tk, "NDX") or str.contains(tk, "NAS") ? "SP:SPX" : "NASDAQ:NDX") :
          typ == "stock"   ? "AMEX:SPY" :
          str.contains(tk, "NAS") or str.contains(tk, "US100") ? "TVC:SPX" : "TVC:NDX"
corr = autoPair ? autoSym : corrIn

[cH, cL, cC] = request.security(corr, timeframe.period, [high, low, close],
     lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)

// A comparison of a symbol with itself produces a flat line and zero
// divergences — which looks broken rather than unconfigured. The panel is
// told to say so.
sameSym = str.contains(syminfo.tickerid, corr) or str.contains(corr, syminfo.ticker)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  CORRELATION
//  The reading that decides whether anything below means anything at all.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
cor = ta.correlation(close, cC, corLen)
corOK = not na(cor) and math.abs(cor) >= corMin

// Inverse pairs are legitimate SMT pairs — EURUSD against DXY is the classic
// one — but their swings are mirrored: my high lines up with their LOW. So
// when the correlation is negative the script reads their lows at my highs
// and their highs at my lows, and asks whether they confirmed in the mirror.
// Without this a negatively correlated pair reports every confirmation as a
// divergence and every divergence as a confirmation.
inv = not na(cor) and cor < 0

// The correlated symbol's own average bar range, used as the yardstick for
// how much of a move counts as real disagreement. Taken from the requested
// highs and lows rather than a second security call — one request is enough.
cRange = ta.sma(cH - cL, 14)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  THE CORRELATED PATH, RESCALED
//  The second symbol's close mapped onto your chart's range over a rolling
//  window. The result carries no price — only shape — which is the entire
//  point: two shapes side by side show the disagreement that a marker can
//  only assert.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
myHi = ta.highest(high, ghostW)
myLo = ta.lowest(low, ghostW)
coHi = ta.highest(cH, ghostW)
coLo = ta.lowest(cL, ghostW)

ghostY = ghost and not na(cC) and coHi > coLo and myHi > myLo ?
         myLo + (cC - coLo) / (coHi - coLo) * (myHi - myLo) : na

// two passes: a soft wide one behind and the line itself on top, so the
// correlated path reads clearly without competing with the candles
plot(ghostY, "Correlated glow", color = color.new(GHOST, 82), linewidth = ghostLw + 3)
plot(ghostY, "Correlated path", color = color.new(GHOST, 15), linewidth = ghostLw)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  CANDLES
//  A matched neon pair with translucent bodies against solid outlines. The
//  background shows faintly through the body while the edge stays crisp,
//  which is what makes a candle read as part of the chart rather than a
//  sticker placed over it. The grey correlated path then sits clearly behind
//  them instead of competing with a default red and green.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// palettes as published, not nudged by eye afterwards
cBull = switch candSty
    "Neon Bloom"    => #00F5D4
    "Midnight Cyan" => #34E89E
    "Vibrant Neon"  => #00E5B0
    "Classic Soft"  => #3FB68B
    "Cool Blue"     => #4C9AFF
    => #00F5D4

cBear = switch candSty
    "Neon Bloom"    => #FF2E88
    "Midnight Cyan" => #FF4D7D
    "Vibrant Neon"  => #FF2E63
    "Classic Soft"  => #E0556B
    "Cool Blue"     => #8A94A6
    => #FF2E88

cEdge = close >= open ? cBull : cBear
plotcandle(candSty == "Off" ? na : open, high, low, close, "Candles",
     color = color.new(cEdge, 24), wickcolor = color.new(cEdge, 18), bordercolor = cEdge)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  HELPERS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
sizeOf(string s) =>
    switch s
        "Small"  => size.small
        "Large"  => size.large
        "Huge"   => size.huge
        => size.normal

hudP(string s) =>
    switch s
        "Top Right"    => position.top_right
        "Top Left"     => position.top_left
        "Bottom Right" => position.bottom_right
        "Bottom Left"  => position.bottom_left
        => position.middle_right

meter(float v) =>
    n = int(math.round(math.min(math.max(v, 0.0), 1.0) * 10))
    s = ""
    for i = 1 to 10
        s := s + (i <= n ? "█" : "░")
    s

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  PIVOTS AND DIVERGENCE
//  A pivot is confirmed, so it only exists once the bars on both sides of it
//  have closed. At each new pivot the script compares the direction your
//  symbol moved since its previous pivot with the direction the correlated
//  symbol moved over the same two bars. When the two signs disagree, that is
//  the divergence.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ph = ta.pivothigh(pivLen, pivLen)
pl = ta.pivotlow(pivLen, pivLen)

// The correlated symbol's swing rarely lands on exactly the same bar as mine,
// so its extreme is taken over a three-bar window centred on my pivot rather
// than read off one bar. The window is offset by pivLen - 1, which keeps its
// most recent bar at least one closed bar back — nothing from the forming bar
// can leak into a confirmed pivot.
coHiWin = ta.highest(cH, 3)[pivLen - 1]
coLoWin = ta.lowest(cL, 3)[pivLen - 1]

var float prevPH  = na      // my previous pivot high
var float prevPHc = na      // the correlated symbol's high at that same bar
var int   prevPHx = na
var float prevPL  = na
var float prevPLc = na
var int   prevPLx = na

var int nBear = 0
var int nBull = 0

// What the panel actually needs: the most recent divergence, which way it
// pointed and how long ago — not a lifetime tally. And how many divergences
// the correlation gate has refused, so that a chart showing marks under a
// WEAK CORRELATION header explains itself: those marks were placed when the
// correlation at that bar was strong enough; the reading has since dropped.
var int lastBar = na
var int lastDir = 0
var int nGated  = 0

// If the correlation changes sign between two pivots, the stored value is a
// low and the new one is a high, or the reverse, and comparing them means
// nothing. The mode is remembered with the pivot, and a change of mode
// discards the stored pivot rather than comparing across it.
var bool prevInvH = false
var bool prevInvL = false

bearSMT = false
bullSMT = false

if not na(ph)
    myNow = ph
    // at my high: their high for a direct pair, their LOW for an inverse one
    coNow = inv ? coLoWin : coHiWin
    xNow  = bar_index - pivLen
    if not na(prevPH) and not na(prevPHc) and not na(coNow) and inv == prevInvH
        myUp = myNow > prevPH
        // did they confirm? Direct pair: a higher high. Inverse: a lower low.
        coConf = inv ? coNow < prevPHc : coNow > prevPHc
        // One made the new extreme, the other did not — and the one that did
        // not has to have travelled far enough for that to be a statement
        // rather than a rounding error against its own average range.
        big  = not na(cRange) and cRange > 0 and math.abs(coNow - prevPHc) >= cRange * minDiv
        found = myUp != coConf and big
        if found and (corOK or not corGate)
            bearSMT := true
            nBear += 1
            lastBar := xNow
            lastDir := -1
            if showLink and xNow - prevPHx <= maxSpan
                line.new(prevPHx, prevPH, xNow, myNow, color = color.new(DN, 10), width = 3, style = line.style_dashed)
            // neon text on a dark ink card: dark ink on a saturated neon fill
            // was the reverse of this and the word disappeared into the colour.
            // Anchored at the pivot price itself, not abovebar — abovebar adds
            // its own gap and the mark floats away from the swing it names
            label.new(xNow, myNow, "▼ SMT", yloc = yloc.price,
                 style = label.style_label_down, color = INK, textcolor = DN, size = sizeOf(lblSize))
        else if found
            nGated += 1
    prevPH   := myNow
    prevPHc  := coNow
    prevPHx  := xNow
    prevInvH := inv

if not na(pl)
    myNow = pl
    // at my low: their low for a direct pair, their HIGH for an inverse one
    coNow = inv ? coHiWin : coLoWin
    xNow  = bar_index - pivLen
    if not na(prevPL) and not na(prevPLc) and not na(coNow) and inv == prevInvL
        myDn = myNow < prevPL
        coConf = inv ? coNow > prevPLc : coNow < prevPLc
        big  = not na(cRange) and cRange > 0 and math.abs(coNow - prevPLc) >= cRange * minDiv
        found = myDn != coConf and big
        if found and (corOK or not corGate)
            bullSMT := true
            nBull += 1
            lastBar := xNow
            lastDir := 1
            if showLink and xNow - prevPLx <= maxSpan
                line.new(prevPLx, prevPL, xNow, myNow, color = color.new(UP, 10), width = 3, style = line.style_dashed)
            label.new(xNow, myNow, "▲ SMT", yloc = yloc.price,
                 style = label.style_label_up, color = INK, textcolor = UP, size = sizeOf(lblSize))
        else if found
            nGated += 1
    prevPL   := myNow
    prevPLc  := coNow
    prevPLx  := xNow
    prevInvL := inv

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  DASHBOARD
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
var table hud = table.new(position.top_right, 2, 6)
if showHUD and barstate.islast
    table.delete(hud)
    hud := table.new(hudP(hudPos), 2, 6, bgcolor = CARD,
         border_color = color.new(#28323F, 0), border_width = 1)

    cAbs = na(cor) ? 0.0 : math.abs(cor)

    // The header answers the only question that matters first: is the last
    // divergence bearish, bullish, or is there none — and how fresh is it.
    ago  = na(lastBar) ? -1 : bar_index - lastBar
    // A divergence is a reversal cue, and a cue from two hundred bars back is
    // history, not a state. Past a few swings the header stops shouting the
    // direction and simply records it — the colour is what a glance reads.
    fresh = ago >= 0 and ago <= math.max(20, pivLen * 4)
    hTx  = sameSym ? "PICK A CORRELATED SYMBOL" :
           lastDir == 0 ? "NO DIVERGENCE YET" :
           fresh ? (lastDir == -1 ? "▼  BEARISH SMT" : "▲  BULLISH SMT") :
           (lastDir == -1 ? "▼  last SMT bearish" : "▲  last SMT bullish")
    hSub = sameSym ? "same symbol  " : ago < 0 ? "  " : str.tostring(ago) + " bars ago  "
    hBg  = sameSym ? #4A3410 : not fresh ? CARD2 : lastDir == -1 ? #8E1148 : #0A5F55
    hFg  = fresh or sameSym ? color.white : lastDir == -1 ? DN : lastDir == 1 ? UP : MUTE

    table.cell(hud, 0, 0, "  " + hTx + "  ", text_color = hFg, text_size = size.large, text_halign = text.align_left, bgcolor = hBg)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 0, hSub, text_color = fresh ? color.white : MUTE, text_size = size.normal, text_halign = text.align_right, bgcolor = hBg)

    table.cell(hud, 0, 1, "  " + syminfo.ticker + "  vs  " + corr + (autoPair ? "  · auto" : ""), text_color = MUTE, text_size = size.normal, text_halign = text.align_left, bgcolor = CARD)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 1, (inv ? "inverse pair" : "direct pair") + "  ", text_color = MUTE, text_size = size.normal, text_halign = text.align_right, bgcolor = CARD)

    // "now" is spelled out: marks already on the chart were placed when the
    // correlation at THAT bar cleared the threshold, and the reading since
    // may have fallen — a weak number here does not un-earn old marks
    corTx = na(cor) ? "—" : str.tostring(cor, "0.00")
    table.cell(hud, 0, 2, "  Correlation now", text_color = MUTE, text_size = size.normal, text_halign = text.align_left, bgcolor = CARD2)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 2, corTx + "  ", text_color = corOK ? TXT : WARN, text_size = size.huge, text_halign = text.align_right, bgcolor = CARD2)

    table.cell(hud, 0, 3, "  " + meter(cAbs), text_color = corOK ? UP : WARN, text_size = size.normal, text_halign = text.align_left, bgcolor = CARD2)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 3, (corOK ? "marks armed" : "marks held — below " + str.tostring(corMin, "0.00")) + "  ", text_color = corOK ? UP : WARN, text_size = size.normal, text_halign = text.align_right, bgcolor = CARD2)

    table.cell(hud, 0, 4, "  Found on this chart", text_color = MUTE, text_size = size.normal, text_halign = text.align_left, bgcolor = CARD)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 4, str.tostring(nBear) + " ▼   " + str.tostring(nBull) + " ▲  ", text_color = TXT, text_size = size.normal, text_halign = text.align_right, bgcolor = CARD)

    table.cell(hud, 0, 5, "  Held back by weak correlation", text_color = MUTE, text_size = size.normal, text_halign = text.align_left, bgcolor = CARD2)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 5, (corGate ? str.tostring(nGated) : "gate off") + "  ", text_color = corGate and nGated > 0 ? WARN : MUTE, text_size = size.normal, text_halign = text.align_right, bgcolor = CARD2)

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  ALERTS — confirmed pivots only
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
alertcondition(bearSMT, "Bearish SMT divergence", "LunqFX SMT: the correlated symbol did not confirm this high")
alertcondition(bullSMT, "Bullish SMT divergence", "LunqFX SMT: the correlated symbol did not confirm this low")
alertcondition(bearSMT or bullSMT, "Any SMT divergence", "LunqFX SMT: a divergence has confirmed against the correlated symbol")
